     1	# Tool Input Validation
     2	
     3	| Field  | Value                |
     4	| ------ | -------------------- |
     5	| Issue  | re-cinq/HALEngine#37 |
     6	| Status | In Progress          |
     7	
     8	Every tool registered in `src/orchestration/tools/registry.ts` declares a JSON Schema `inputSchema` that is forwarded to the configured AI provider, and `ToolRegistry.execute` validates the model-supplied `input` against that schema before calling the tool's executor. When validation fails, `execute` returns a rejection `ToolResponse` describing what was wrong rather than throwing, so the orchestrator feeds the failure back to the model as a `tool_result` within the existing tool-round budget.
     9	
    10	## Validation at execute time
    11	
    12	- When `input` omits a property listed in `required`, `ToolRegistry.execute` returns a `ToolResponse` whose `result` names the missing property as required and never invokes the executor ([validated by: rejects input missing a required property with a ToolResponse and never calls the executor](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L16), [names a missing required property and never calls the executor](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L17)).
    13	- When `input` supplies a property whose value does not match its declared JSON Schema type, `ToolRegistry.execute` returns a `ToolResponse` naming the failing field path and expected type without invoking the executor, and neither `result` nor the emitted log record contains the offending property value ([validated by: rejects a wrong-typed property naming the field and expected type without leaking the offending value to the result or logs](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L46), [names a nested wrong-typed field by dotted path and expected type without echoing its value](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L32)).
    14	- When `input` satisfies `required`, declared property types and `enum`, `ToolRegistry.execute` invokes the executor once and passes the input object through unchanged — including undeclared extra properties — without injecting `additionalProperties: false`, coercing types or applying defaults.
    15	- When `ToolRegistry.execute` is called with a tool name that has not been registered, it throws `Unknown tool: <name>` because an unregistered tool is a programming or configuration error rather than a recoverable input mistake.
    16	
    17	## Schema compilation and caching
    18	
    19	- A static `ToolDefinition` has its `inputSchema` compiled once at `register` time, so a schema that is not valid JSON Schema throws synchronously from `register` with an error message naming the offending tool ([validated by: throws at register time naming the tool when a static inputSchema cannot be compiled](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L94), [throws naming the tool when a static inputSchema has a malformed required list](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L49)).
    20	- A static tool's `inputSchema` is compiled at `register` time and reused on every `execute` call without re-reading or re-compiling `definition.inputSchema`.
    21	- A function-valued `ToolDefinitionSource` resolves its definition on each `execute` call and validates against the `inputSchema` returned for that call, caching compiled validators by schema object reference (`WeakMap`) so distinct returned schemas each enforce their own rules.
    22	
    23	## Orchestrator tool-loop recovery
    24	
    25	- Because a schema rejection returns a `ToolResponse` instead of throwing, `src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.ts` sends the rejection `result` back to the provider as a `tool_result` message, allowing the model to issue a corrected tool call on the next round and complete the turn within `maxToolRounds` ([validated by: returns a schema rejection as a tool_result in the orchestrator loop so the model can correct the call on the next round](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L112)).
# Lore test-command interface (lore_list_tests / lore_run_test).
# Written for this repo's Jest suite rather than adapted from the Vitest
# equivalent for a Vitest suite: the report shapes happen to match, but the
# invocations do not.
- id: hal-engine-jest
  name: HAL Engine Jest suite
  cwd: .
  description: >-
    The ts-jest unit suite over src/ and scripts/ (jest.config.js roots), run
    on every pull request in .github/workflows/ci.yml as the "Test" step.
    Selectors are "<repo-relative-file>::<full test name>" as emitted by the
    list command. Jest has no dry-run, so the list command executes the suite
    once with --json and derives every name from the report; it deliberately
    tolerates a red suite, since listing must not depend on the tests passing.
    The run command selects one test with --runTestsByPath plus an anchored,
    regex-escaped -t, and treats a selector that matches nothing as a failure
    rather than a green run of an empty suite. Neither command builds first:
    no test here reads dist/.
  list: node scripts/lore-list-tests.mjs
  run: node scripts/lore-run-test.mjs "{selector}"
  coverage_format: lcov

